Title: "Labels"
Id: "62a24a65-05c9-4e09-9722-1eb355fe2ce5"

Text: "Labels identify items inside brackets or nested groups.

```mlg-fragment
[\theorem.ordinal.trichotomy]
Theorem:
then: ...
```

Label headers are parsed from dotted parts. Dots are commonly rendered as spaces in fallback display names, with words capitalized for theorem-like and axiom-like cards.

Mathlingua provides four ways to define a label on code or prose, and one unified syntax `(:label:)` to refer to a label:

1. Structural Label Headers: Square-bracketed headings before a top-level group or nested clause group (such as `[theorem.name]` or `[step]`).

2. Line-Level Labels: Attached to single-line formulations or text items using `. [label]: ...`:

```mlg-fragment
then:
. [base_case]: x > 0
. [note]: \"Check inductive step next\"
```

Line labels must appear on their own indented line; inline arguments (such as `forAll: [l]: x > 0`) cannot have labels.

3. Inline Formulation Labels: Attached to grouped formulations using `[:label:]` or multi-labels `[:l1, l2:]`:

```mlg-fragment
(x + y)[:sum:]
(.x is \real.)[:typed, positive:]
```

4. Labeled Prose Quotes: Attached to quoted prose in `OpenText` using `&#39;&#39;text&#39;&#39;[&#58;label&#58;]`.

5. Label References (`(:label:)`): Used to refer to a defined label `label` in `Justification:` (`. for: (:label:) / have: ...`), `References:` (`. for: (:label:) / entry: $resource`), or prose (`(:label:)`).

Unlike structural label headers, inline formulation labels `[:a.b:]` and label references `(:a.b:)` do not support stropped symbolic parts."
Id: "d36979a8-c1ec-4f3b-8e8c-3dafd5ee366b"
